OK, I managed to pinpoint the heart of the issue. Using your data, I have produced the following:

Setdata <- read.csv("20-03-26-SetData_GROW.csv", sep = ";", dec = ",", row.names = 1)
ttGROW <- truthTable(data = Setdata, outcome = "GROW",

              conditions = "FINBIG, SUBS, FFF, PERS, BUSINESS, EO",
              incl.cut = 0.79, n.cut = 2, sort.by <http://sort.by> = "incl, n", complete=TRUE,

              show.cases = TRUE, dcc = TRUE, use.letters = TRUE)
cs <- minimize(ttGROW, all.sol = TRUE, row.dom = TRUE)
ps <- minimize(ttGROW, include = "?", all.sol = TRUE, row.dom = TRUE)
is <- minimize(ttGROW, include = "?", all.sol = TRUE, row.dom = TRUE,
          dir.exp = "FINBIG, SUBS, FFF, PERS, BUSINESS, EO")

In the truthTable() command, I added use.letters = TRUE to make it look easier to follow, with the following correspndence:
A: FINBIG
B: SUBS
C: FFF
D: PERS
E: BUSINESS
F: EO

This is the CS model no.21:
M21: ~A*~B*~D*E + ~A*B*C*F + A*~B*~C*~E*F + A*~B*C*~E*~F + B*C*D*E*F +
     ~A*C*E*~F + ~A*B*~C*~D*~F + ~A*B*~C*~E*~F + ~A*B*D*~E*F + ~B*~C*D*~E*F +
     ~B*C*D*~E*~F => GROW

This is the PS model no.20:
M21: ~D*E + B*C*E + ~C*D*~E*F + ~B*C*~D + C*D*~F + ~A*B*C*F + ~A*B*D*~E +
     A*~C*~E*F + B*~C*~D*~F => GROW

This is the IS model for this pair, in version 3.7:
M1: ~D*E + B*C*E + C*D*~F + ~A*B*C*F + A*~C*~E*F + ~C*D*~E*F + ~A*B*~C*~E*~F +
    A*~B*C*~E*~F => GROW

And these are the two models from version 3.6:
M1: ~D*E + B*C*E + C*D*~F + ~A*B*C*F + A*~C*~E*F + ~C*D*~E*F + ~A*B*~C*~E*~F +
    A*~B*C*~F => GROW
M2: ~D*E + B*C*E + C*D*~F + ~A*B*C*F + A*~B*C*~F + A*~C*~E*F + ~C*D*~E*F +
    ~A*B*D*~E + B*~C*~D*~F => GROW

Let us look at your yellow terms (purple here): ~A*B*~C*~D*~F (in the CS) and B*~C*~D*~F (in the PS model)
As it turns out, the term B*~C*~D*~F (on the 8th row in the PI chart below) is redundant because it is not
minimally needed to solve the PI chart:

is$i.sol$C21P20$PIchart

                3  4  6  11 12 13 15 17 19 21 22 26 27 28 30 31 32 34 38 41 45 64
~D*E            x  x  -  x  x  -  -  -  x  -  -  -  x  x  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -
B*C*E           -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  x  x  -  x  x  -  -  -  -  x
C*D*~F          -  -  -  -  -  x  x  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  x  -  -  -  -  x  -
C*E*~F          -  -  -  x  -  -  x  -  -  -  -  -  x  -  -  x  -  -  -  -  -  -
~A*B*C*D        -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  x  x  x  -  -  -  -  -
~A*B*C*F        -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  x  -  x  x  -  x  -  -  -  -  -
A*~C*~E*F       -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  x  x  -  -  -
B*~C*~D*~F      -  -  -  -  -  -  -  x  x  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -
~C*D*~E*F       -  -  x  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  x  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  x  -  -  -
~A*B*~C*~E*~F   -  -  -  -  -  -  -  x  -  x  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -
~A*B*D*~E*F     -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  x  -  -  -  x  -  -  -  -  -  -  -
A*~B*C*~E*~F    -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  -  x  x  -

solveChart(is$i.sol$C21P20$PIchart)
     [,1]
[1,]    1
[2,]    2
[3,]    3
[4,]    6
[5,]    7
[6,]    9
[7,]   10
[8,]   12

So the term is indeed found in the prime implicants, but just redundant for the purpose of the intermediate
solution. The same happens with your red items, which in the PI chart is found on the 11th line. They are simply made redundant by the other intermediate solution terms. You have only obtained that model in version 3.6 because of activating the argument all.sol, however there is no real added value in a non-minimal intermediate solution.

To quote from my presentation in Zurich, we have model ambiguity in the CS, we have model ambiguity in the PS, and we also have model ambiguity on the IS, which means ambiguity generated by ambiguity. If anything, that is just too much ambiguity, IMHO.

About the neg.out = TRUE, I realised later that it is misplaced in the minimize() command. That is not a formal argument for the minimization, but only for the truth table creation. But since you have already created the truth table (ttGROW) and used that as an input for the minimization step, activating neg.out is simply ignored.

Does this explains your results?
Best,
Adrian

